Coding agents write and push faster than most CI systems were designed to
serve. The open problem is verification: how do you keep a fail-closed merge
gate when the suite already ran on a laptop or agent machine, and when the
cloud re-run is either too expensive or too slow?

Builders who live in that loop report Agents multiplying micro-PRs while
[GitHub Actions](https://docs.github.com/en/actions) re-runs the same suite on
branch and main, sometimes until free-tier minutes run out and merges freeze
with local tests still green. Industry reporting around AI-generated code keeps
landing on the same gap: high distrust of AI output and uneven verification
before merge (Sonar’s 2026 State of Code framing, widely discussed as
“verification debt”).

**thwip** is our answer so far, in public beta. It is a control plane and a small
protocol for *commit-carried CI evidence*: run approved checks where the code
lives, sign a compact proof of the exact Git tree, and let a ruleset-protected
GitHub App verify that proof before merge. We treat this as research in
verifiable agent coding, with a working product attached so the claims stay
falsifiable.

