## Related work and inspiration

We stand on existing supply-chain and attestation work rather than inventing a
parallel universe of crypto primitives.

- **[in-toto](https://in-toto.io/)** and its
  [Statement](https://github.com/in-toto/attestation/tree/main/spec/v1) shape:
  how we carry a typed predicate over a subject.
- **[DSSE](https://github.com/secure-systems-lab/dsse)** (Dead Simple Signing
  Envelope): how the proof is signed and transported inside a git trailer.
- **[SLSA](https://slsa.dev/)**, especially
  [Build L3](https://slsa.dev/spec/v1.0/levels#build-l3): the bar we claim for
  the **remote** profile when GitHub runs and signs the suites. Fast is
  deliberately not L3; see below.
- **[Sigstore](https://www.sigstore.dev/)** and related keyless signing
  ecosystems: we did *not* adopt keyless identity for the first slice. Device
  keys enrolled on GitHub keep the trust story inspectable and offline-capable
  for local runs.
- **[Dagger](https://dagger.io/)**: content-addressed pipelines so “same tree,
  same digests” is a property of the graph, not a wish.
- **[GitHub Apps](https://docs.github.com/en/apps)** and
  [rulesets](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-rulesets/about-rulesets):
  App-native checks without putting a repository workflow in the trust boundary
  that judges the candidate (see our
  [ADR on App-native proof checks](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/blob/main/docs/adr/0001-app-native-proof-checks.md)).
- **Provenance and attestations in CI** more broadly: GitHub’s own
  [artifact attestations](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/security-for-github-actions/using-artifact-attestations/using-artifact-attestations-to-establish-provenance-for-builds)
  and the OCI/supply-chain literature. thwip targets a different artifact: the
  *commit itself* as the carrier for CI evidence about agent-local work.
- **Local signoff culture** (self-hosted runners, `bin/signoff`-style workflows):
  the social move to stop paying the cloud to re-prove work you already ran.
  thwip’s job is to keep that evidence something a merge queue can still
  require.

If you know adjacent systems we should cite or compare, open an issue. Related
work is part of the research surface.

## The edge verification thesis

Agents and developers already execute lint, typecheck, and tests on the machine
that holds the checkout, the toolchain, and the agent session. That run is the
natural place to produce evidence. The merge authority still needs something it
can require without replaying the world.

thwip’s thesis is that those two roles can stay separate:

1. **Execution at the edge** produces digests and a signed statement over a
   fixed tree and policy.
2. **Verification at the platform** checks the signature, the subject, current
   GitHub permission, key enrollment, and policy version, and publishes a check
   the ruleset can pin.

The agent may drive the CLI. An enrolled device key signs. The App evaluates.
We keep the agent outside the trust boundary that decides pass or fail. That
split is the research claim as much as any feature.

## Minimal trust setup (what works in beta)

The beta implements a complete, narrow loop.

**Carrier.** Evidence is a git trailer `thwip-Proof: v1.<base64url DSSE
envelope>` over an in-toto Statement v1 with predicate
`urn:thwip:attestation:ci-run:v1`. The payload is compact enough to ride the
commit.

**Signing.** An OpenSSH ed25519 key, enrolled as a GitHub
[Signing key](https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/connecting-to-github-with-ssh/checking-for-existing-ssh-keys)
for the same account the CLI authenticates as, signs the envelope. Device
registration is a human step on purpose. Authentication keys do not count.

**Verification.** The thwip GitHub App owns the required `thwip` check. It
evaluates the exact PR or merge-group subject, uses the shared verifier
package, and executes no repository code. It reads GitHub for permission and
keys, fetches a signed fact bundle from the control plane, and fails closed with
stable reason codes (`PASS`, `SUBJECT_TREE_MISMATCH`, `SIGNER_KEY_REVOKED`, and
siblings).

**Policy.** Organization defaults live in the dashboard. An optional
`thwip.yaml` on the active default branch may override them after protected
merge. Configuration on the candidate pull request never approves that pull
request.

**Profiles.**

| Profile | Who ran the suites | What the merge can cite |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **fast** | Signer on their machine | Head proof; no pre-run control-plane contact |
| **remote** | GitHub Actions on the integration candidate | GitHub-signed suite results (SLSA Build L3 for those checks) |

`fresh` and `audited` appear in the schema as names for assignment plus
integration receipt. Nothing issues or signs those yet, so the CLI and control
plane refuse them by name rather than quietly delivering remote under a
stronger label.

**Queue.** On this repository we dogfood a merge queue that records digests you
can fetch and re-check (`GET /v1/queue/records/:digest`). A merge record cites
what was verified; if a required citation is missing, the queue refuses to sign
a hollow record. Design notes live under
[`docs/`](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/tree/main/docs) and
[`THWIP.md`](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/blob/main/THWIP.md).

That is the minimal setup we claim works today: edge execution, commit-carried
proof, App-native check, GitHub-mirrored membership, two honest profiles.

## What a proof does not buy you

Precision is the whole point of this note.

- thwip **does not observe execution**. Under fast, the signature is an
  attributed claim that the approved suites passed on that tree. Detection of
  lies is reproducibility and disagreement across machines, later commits, and
  remote runs.
- thwip **never sees source**. The App and control plane verify metadata and
  digests; they do not fetch a worktree to re-run your code for the proof
  check.
- A green `thwip` check is **not** a guarantee of product correctness, security
  review, or end-to-end user behavior. It is a structured claim about approved
  suite digests and who signed them.
- Local and remote environments still diverge. Paths that need the cloud as the
  execution environment should use **remote**. Auth, billing, deploy config, and
  lockfiles are the usual candidates.
- Identity is **GitHub’s**. Membership and repository permission come from the
  org and collaborators. We add no second invite graph.

Short forms for humans and agents: [FAQ](/faq), [faq.md](/faq.md),
[llms.txt](/llms.txt).

## Framework sketch

```
edge (agent / developer machine)
  thwip run → suite digests → sign → amend trailer

git
  commit carries thwip-Proof over tree T, policy P, signer S

platform (GitHub + thwip App + control plane)
  require thwip check → verify envelope against T, P, S, live GitHub facts
  optional: queue cites digests for the merge record
```

Content-addressed pipelines (Dagger modules, named required functions) make
“same tree, same digests” a real property. The control plane is policy, keys,
and verification. It is not a CI farm.

## Open questions

We opened the beta so others can stress the model on real agent workflows and
help expand it.

- Integration receipts and assignment so `fresh` / `audited` mean something
  distinct from remote.
- Stronger multi-party agreement on digests before merge.
- Hybrid matrix ergonomics when part of the suite must stay remote (for example
  macOS or Windows legs).
- Tooling that helps agents fail closed *before* push without inventing a
  second policy language.
- Independent verification of published queue records and chain links.
- Comparison write-ups against keyless signing, build provenance only, and
  pure self-hosted Actions.

The monorepo is public:
[github.com/recallnet/thwip](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip). Issues and PRs
are welcome. This repository ships with thwip on its own merge path, so
contributions meet the same evidence bar we describe.

## Try the working slice

1. `npm install -g @recallnet/thwip` (Node 22+), package
   [`@recallnet/thwip`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@recallnet/thwip).
2. `thwip login`, then register an ed25519 key as a GitHub **Signing** key.
3. Install the App, enable a project, `thwip init`, `thwip run`, push.

